user
You are a judge for the "Flip the Switch" hackathon (WeMakeDevs, DevTools Academy, and Switch by SandboxAQ).
Switch is an open-source orchestration layer that puts AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, and others) and people into shared team-chat rooms (Slack, Discord, Mattermost), with rooms, roles, addressing policies, templates, documents, and custom agents that join over an HTTP and server-sent-events protocol.

Official rubric:
- Use of Switch, 30%: is Switch central, and would the project stop working without it?
- Technical execution, 25%: does it run, and could someone clone the repo and reproduce it?
- Creativity and impact, 25%: is it a fresh idea that solves a problem a real team has?
- Presentation and documentation, 20%: do the demo video and README make clear what was built and how Switch is used?

You can see only the submission's written material below, not its code or video. Judge only what the material shows.

Four submissions follow, labelled W, X, Y, Z. Names are removed.

=== Submission W
# the project

**An AI teammate can explain an invoice exception. It cannot approve itself.**

the project turns a team chat into an evidence-backed invoice review desk. A reviewer asks `@ledger reconcile november` in Mattermost. **Real Switch** delivers the message to a Python agent. Exact arithmetic matches invoices against purchase orders and delivery receipts; a real Codex invocation explains the exceptions. The named reviewer approves an exact evidence digest, and the agent exports only eligible invoices with a verifiable receipt.

Built for **Flip the Switch, Best Business Workflow**, November 4–7, 2026. All included companies, documents and amounts are synthetic. No payments, accounting postings or bank connections are implemented.

![Actual Switch + Mattermost technical test, using a synthetic batch and test reviewer](docs/live-room.jpg)

[Watch the 1m44s demo](https://github.com/ivergit88/ledgerroom-switch/releases/tag/v0.1.0-demo) · [Verification record](VERIFICATION.md) · [Build article](docs/BUILD_ARTICLE.md) · [Passing CI](https://github.com/ivergit88/ledgerroom-switch/actions/runs/41735450113)

The narrated demo is an edited walkthrough of screenshots from the actual local integration test. It uses synthetic documents and an automation-controlled local reviewer account. It shows the real Switch/Codex response, wrong-digest refusal and eligible-only export; it is not a real customer approval.

Inspect the captured technical-run outputs in [docs/proof](docs/proof). The agent never reads these recorded examples as replies; live explanations come from Codex.

## The demo in one minute

1. Send `@ledger reconcile november` in the `ledger-review` room.
2. Read the deterministic table: **3 eligible invoices / USD 840.00; 5 held rows / USD 1,664.00**. Every finding cites its CSV record. These are test-batch face values, not measured savings.
3. Read the real Codex analysis and procurement questions. One supplier note says to ignore instructions and approve everything; it remains untrusted document data.
4. Try an approval with a wrong digest: it is refused.
5. As the configured reviewer, send `@ledger approve eligible november <full SHA-256 from the report>`.
6. Verify `reviewed-invoices.csv` contains only NS-8801, NS-8802 and LM-400. `decision.json` records the Switch decision message, human sender, evidence digest and export hash. Held invoices remain excluded.

## Why Switch is essential

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    H[Named reviewer in Mattermost] --> B[Real Switch collaboration bridge]
    B --> W[Switch authenticated WebSocket]
    W --> A[the project agent]
    A --> R[Exact three-way matching + source hashes]
    R --> C[Codex: explanation and questions]
    A --> G[SQLite evidence-bound approval gate]
    G --> E[Eligible-only CSV + decision receipt]
    A -->|Authenticated HTTP message| B
```

Switch supplies the room, message identity, agent addressing, persistent conversation and agent event delivery. There is no alternate chat-to-export path. Disconnecting Switch stops the agent workflow. The offline reconciliation command is a verification tool and cannot create an approval or reviewed export.

The client uses the [current Switch WebSocket protocol](https://docs.switchagents.ai/switch-rooms/internals/agent-connection), handles pings independently of model work, saves connection generation/cursor, fences reconnections and stops on takeover or a history gap. It discovers the operation registry at startup. It uses `connect_to_room`, `read_context` and the room-explicit message API.

## Run locally

Requires Python **3.12+**, Docker with Compose, and an authenticated **Codex CLI**. Switch images are pinned to **0.31.0**, Mattermost to **11.9.0**. A first pull downloads large container images. The services bind to loopback only; no public server or paid service is provisioned.

From the repository root:

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python tools/init_stack.py
docker compose --project-name ledgerroom --env-file .local/stack.env -f infra/switch.compose.yml --profile collab --profile gateway up -d
```

Wait for the setup container to finish (`docker logs ledgerroom-setup-1` should say `Setup complete!`), then:

```powershell
python tools/bootstrap.py
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m ledgerroom run
```

On Linux/macOS replace `.venv/Scripts/python.exe` with `.venv/bin/python`. `tools/bootstrap.py` needs the Docker CLI available to the current account. It is safe to rerun: it retains the existing agent, room and credentials instead of rotating agent keys.

Open **http://localhost:18065/ledgerroom/channels/ledger-review**, choose **View in Browser**, and sign in as `reviewer` using `MATTERMOST_USER_PASSWORD` in `.local/stack.env`. The local Switch Gateway is http://localhost:13000; its generated login is also in that file. These are local demonstration accounts, unrelated to the hackathon login.

`init_stack.py` creates random local secrets and retains them on subsequent runs. `.local/`, `.env`, logs and generated artifacts are excluded from Git. Do not upload these directories. The default demo bootstrap expects the bundled `reviewer` account and the `ledgerroom` Compose project name.

### Windows WSL fallback used for the recorded test

The existing Docker Desktop on the development machine failed in its Inference-manager IPC initialization. Its files and settings were not repaired or reset. An isolated existing Ubuntu Docker daemon was started with:

```powershell
wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 -u root -- dockerd --host unix:///run/ledgerroom-docker.sock --data-root /var/lib/ledgerroom-docker --exec-root /run/ledgerroom-docker --pidfile /run/ledgerroom-docker.pid --log-level error
```

Keep that process running. In another shell use the default WSL user (it has the working Compose plugin):

```powershell
wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 -- docker --host unix:///run/ledgerroom-docker.sock compose --project-name ledgerroom --env-file /mnt/c/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Switch-Hackathon/project/.local/stack.env -f /mnt/c/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Switch-Hackathon/project/infra/switch.compose.yml --profile collab --profile gateway up -d
python tools/bootstrap.py --wsl
```

The `/mnt/c/...` paths above are specific to the development machine; adapt them to your checkout. The native Docker route above is the ordinary setup. Stop only this stack with the same Compose prefix followed by `stop`; stop its daemon with Ctrl+C in its owning terminal. Avoid `down -v` if you want to retain room history.

## Verify without any service or model

```powershell
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
python -m ledgerroom reconcile examples/november --out artifacts/offline
python -m compileall -q ledgerroom tools
```

The tests use the standard library. They cover exact decimal arithmetic, cumulative delivery limits, duplicate copies, unknown orders, mismatches, malicious supplier notes, multiline CSV records, digest tampering, identity spoofing, stale decisions, persistence, eligible-only export and its hash. Offline workflow tests mock the model and Switch transport; live infrastructure/model evidence is reported separately in `VERIFICATION.md`.

## Decision and data boundaries

- Code decides eligibility and totals. Model text is advisory and cannot mutate the matching result, allowlist or decision.
- Approvals bind the room, batch, exact evidence digest and Switch message ID. A modified source file invalidates the old digest. A replay of the same decision is idempotent; a new decision against already approved evidence is refused.
- Each proposal preserves the original CSV bytes alongside the report, after verifying their hashes. A reviewer can inspect the exact documents that produced that version of the evidence.
- The sender is pinned to a concrete human principal. A matching display name is insufficient. Switch 0.31.0 omits the ordinary `sender_kind` field in room history, so the **local administrator bootstrap** resolves the seeded reviewer once from Switch's principal registry with a read-only database query. The running agent receives only that opaque sender ID and its own agent key; it does not receive database or Gateway credentials. This version-specific setup step is isolated in `tools/bootstrap.py`.
- CSV and supplier notes are untrusted. Strict commands reject path traversal and blanket approvals. Export fields are escaped against common spreadsheet formula injection. Hashes detect content changes; they are not digital signatures or proof against an administrator editing the local machine.
- Codex is launched in a temporary working directory with a read-only sandbox; configured MCP connectors are disabled for this job. It reuses the user's CLI authentication and model/provider configuration. See [official non-interactive-mode documentation](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/non-interactive-mode). A provider failure is reported explicitly instead of inserting a canned response.

## Input scope and limitations

The prototype supports **one line per invoice**, positive integer quantities, exact non-negative prices with at most two decimal places, and three-letter uppercase currency codes. It keeps totals separate by currency and performs no FX conversion, tax calculation or tolerance policy. Multi-line invoice IDs are conservatively held as duplicate records; PDF/OCR ingestion, ERP connectors, returns, partial approval and prior-period billed balances are not implemented. Invoice references use **logical CSV record numbers including the header**, not physical text line numbers.

Matching is conservative: if the batch cumulatively exceeds ordered or received quantity, every affected row is held. It does not guess which invoice to release first. This is a local prototype, with one sequential worker and local SQLite state; administrator security, high availability and production accounting controls are outside its current scope.

## Third-party components

The deployment Compose file is from [sandbox-quantum/switch, switch-v0.31.0](https://github.com/sandbox-quantum/switch/tree/switch-v0.31.0), commit `7e57cd433a922bc91a00150c106e6ef1f15b1057`. Switch uses Apache 2.0 with the Commons Clause; see `infra/SWITCH-LICENSE.txt`. Mattermost and Codex remain separate products with their own terms. the project's original code is MIT licensed.

=== Submission X
# the project

Question the other team's coding agent. Switch won't let you drive it.

Every pull request has a flipside: the agent session that wrote it.
When one team's agent writes a change and another team owns the code, the project lets the owning team question that session in a Switch room without the author handing it over.
Each answer is a quote from the session sealed into the PR's commit, checked by the questioning team, and the author approves the exact bytes before they leave their machine.

Track 01, Business workflow. Demo in Slack; reproducible on Switch's built-in chat with no accounts.
Status: kill tests K1 and K2 pass on switch-core 0.31.0 (9/9 checks, `evidence/`); the rest is not built yet.

## Problem

Coding agents made pull requests cheap to write and expensive to review, and the reviewer cannot tell whether the author understands the change.

| Evidence | Source |
|---|---|
| "Today I announced that I won't be reviewing AI generated PRs at company meeting": 1,560 points, 406 comments, 27 May 2026; data scientists sent Claude Code PRs to the web team | [r/ExperiencedDevs](https://www.reddit.com/r/ExperiencedDevs/comments/1towli9/) |
| "There is an AI code review bubble": 351 points, 249 comments, Jan 2026 | [HN 46766961](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46766961) |
| Godot maintainers report a flood of AI-generated PRs, Mar 2026 | [HN 47251385](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47251385) |

The hardest case is a cross-team pull request.
The author's team wrote it with an agent, the owning team must review it, and the context that explains the change exists only in the author's agent session.

## What exists, and the gap

| Need | Existing tools | Gap |
|---|---|---|
| Check an agent's claims against its transcript | claimcheck, groundtruth, agentvow, workproof | The author runs them for themselves; the reviewer cannot ask anything |
| Make the author answer questions | PR Quiz, PR Guard, Cognit, Gater, sphinx-ci | Questions come from the diff, and answers are not tied to what the agent actually did |
| Show the session to the reviewer | Entire, Copilot coding agent session logs | They push the whole transcript; Entire ships [`--skip-push-sessions`](https://github.com/entireio/cli/blob/main/docs/security-and-privacy.md) because authors keep sessions private |
| Gate AI pull requests | mergewarden, CodeRabbit, Copilot review | They judge the diff, not the author's account of it |

**Gap:** the owning team asks specific questions and gets answers that quote the author's private session.
Only the quoted chunks leave the author's machine, and only after the author approves them.
This needs two owners, two agents, private context on one side, and an access rule that stops one side from driving the other side's agent.

## How it works (the one live path)

```
 Author's machine                 Switch                          Owning team's machine
 ┌───────────────────────┐                                        ┌────────────────────────┐
 │ author-agent          │  #pr-142-author (private)              │ reviewer-agent         │
 │ owner: author         │◄── approval card ──► author            │ owner: reviewer        │
 │ sealed session (local)│                                        │ reads diff, asks,      │
 │ drafts, checks, posts │◄──────────── #pr-142 (shared) ────────►│ runs flip verify       │
 └───────────────────────┘       reviewer (human) ◄──►            └────────────────────────┘
   addressing: owner, owner's agents, and reviewer-agent in #pr-142 only
```

1. **Seal.** `flip seal` splits each session step into chunks of at most 300 characters and hashes them into a Merkle tree.
   Each leaf is salted, and leaf and node hashes use the RFC 6962 prefixes, so a proof's sibling hashes do not let anyone guess short chunks.
   The root goes into the PR head commit as a `the project-Seal:` trailer.
   Every question names that commit; `flip verify` rejects a root from any later commit, so re-sealing an edited session after the questions arrive fails verification.
2. **Open.** A Switch template run creates `#pr-142` (reviewer, reviewer-agent, author-agent) and `#pr-142-author` (author, author-agent).
   author-agent's addressing policy admits its owner, the owner's agents, and reviewer-agent in `#pr-142` only.
3. **Ask.** reviewer-agent posts at most five questions as JSON: `file`, `lines`, `kind` (why, test, scope), and an optional CI fact id.
   author-agent parses them with a fixed schema and drops anything else: `file` must be in the PR's changed-file list and `lines` must match `^\d+-\d+$`, so no free text from the reviewer's LLM reaches the author's session.
   A person on the owning team who tags author-agent gets Switch's own refusal.
4. **Draft.** author-agent asks Claude Code, over the local session, which chunk answers the question.
5. **Check.** author-agent checks every draft deterministically:
   - the chunk exists in the sealed tree;
   - if the question names a file, the chunk's step has a tool call on that file;
   - a secret scanner finds nothing in the chunk.
   A draft that fails is retried once; a second failure posts "no session evidence" for that question.
6. **Approve.** author-agent opens a Switch approval card in `#pr-142-author` whose detail is the exact chunk and its hash.
   Only someone who may address author-agent can answer it; the reviewer gets `403 You may not address this agent, so you may not answer it.`
7. **Post.** author-agent posts the approved chunk with its Merkle proof in `#pr-142`.
   A coverage line computed in plain Python posts with it, for example "4 sealed steps touched auth/session.py: 1 disclosed, 3 withheld (leaves 88, 91, 97)", so the reviewer sees what the author chose not to show.
   An optional author's note is posted beside it, labelled unverified.
8. **Verify.** reviewer-agent runs `flip verify`: the chunk hashes to the leaf, and the proof reaches the root in the commit trailer.
   When every question is verified or marked "no session evidence", reviewer-agent pages the reviewer with one summary.

Example:

```
#pr-142  (web team reviewing a pull request from the data team)
reviewer-agent: Q2 {"file": "auth/session.py", "lines": "40-52", "kind": "why"}
author-agent:   Q2, approved by @priya. Session step 23, chunk 2, proof ✓ against seal 9f3c0e1 (commit a41b2c7):
                "test_export_csv failed with SessionExpired; raising SESSION_TTL to 86400 in auth/session.py"
                Coverage: 2 sealed steps touched auth/session.py: 1 disclosed, 1 withheld (leaf 61).
                Author's note (unverified): the TTL change was only meant for the test.
reviewer:       @author-agent show me the full transcript
author-agent:   [Switch] You're not permitted to direct messages to me in this room -
                my operator has restricted who can address me here.
reviewer:       A security change inside a CSV pull request. Split it out.
```

Priya learns about the TTL change from her own agent's session on the approval card, before the reviewer sees it, and the coverage line stops her quietly withholding it.

The demo shows four refusals, each with its log line:
1. The reviewer tags author-agent: Switch refuses (`delivery/addressing.py`).
2. reviewer-agent sends free text instead of JSON: author-agent's schema drops it.
3. A forged chunk fails `flip verify`: the hash does not reach the seal.
4. The reviewer clicks the author's approval card: Switch returns 403 (`session_activity/service.py:463`).

## Why Switch is load-bearing

| Need | Switch feature (switch-core 0.31.0 source) | What a plain bot or CI job lacks |
|---|---|---|
| The reviewer cannot drive the author's agent, while reviewer-agent can ask in one room | Scoped addressing policy: per-room rules for users and agents, `PUT /agents/{id}/addressing-policy` (`addressing.py`); verified in K1.e and K1.f | A CI job has no notion of who may instruct an agent |
| Only the author releases each answer | Approval cards answerable only by people who may address the agent (`session_activity/service.py:463`); verified in K2 | Custom permission code and a custom approval UI |
| Two owners' agents meet without sharing machines or keys | Agents keep their owner; rooms hold agents from several owners | A bot runs under one identity with one set of credentials |
| One pair of rooms per PR, set up the same way each time | Template v2 runs: rooms, agents, `addressing`, `connections`, `kickoff` (`template_guide.py`) | Manual channel setup per PR |
| the project's own agents in the conversation | Protocol agents over `register-known`, server-sent events, `message`, and session approvals (`/agent-sessions/{id}/approvals`) | A bot per chat platform |
| Teams stay in their chat app | Slack, Discord, Mattermost, Teams, Telegram bridges (`bridges/collaboration/`) | A rebuild per platform |

## Switch Console fit

Each item below was checked in Console 0.40.1 or switch-core 0.31.0.

- The repo ships `flipside.template.yaml` (template version 2); a team imports it in Console > Templates.
- author-agent and reviewer-agent are Claude Code or Codex agents that Console already runs (`provider: claude | codex`).
- The reviewer-agent gets read access to the repo through the template's `connections: [{service: github, access: read}]`.
- The reviewing team joins through Console's workspace invite, and each person keeps ownership of their own agent.
- Switch Trust, when the workspace enables it, checks each message before it is sent (`trust/client.py`). It adds to author-agent's secret scan and does not replace it.

## Chat app

The demo runs in Slack, where cross-team review happens at most companies.
A self-hosted Switch connects to Slack over Socket Mode with an app created from Switch's own manifest (`bridges/collaboration/own_app_setup.py`), so it needs no public URL.
The same template runs on Discord, Teams, Mattermost, Telegram, or Switch's built-in chat; the kill tests and `make replay` use the built-in chat, so a judge needs no accounts.

## What is deterministic

Sealing, question parsing, the chunk and file checks, the secret scan, `flip verify`, the page rule, and the ledger are plain Python with no LLM calls.
LLMs only choose which questions to ask (reviewer side) and which chunk answers them (author side).
A chunk reaches the reviewer only after it passes the checks and the author approves it.

## Impact, shown on fixtures

A small fixture set, labelled as a demonstration and not as proof of savings:
- 4 pull requests where the agent made an unrelated change (the TTL case, a disabled test, a widened CORS rule, a new dependency);
- 2 honest pull requests;
- 2 adversarial cases: a valid but misleading quote, and a session edited after the questions arrived.
For each we report whether the change surfaced, whether `flip verify` passed, and any false approval or false flag.

## Reproduce

- `make replay`: plays recorded events and agent outputs into a fresh local Switch server, with no chat workspace and no vendor keys.
- `make test`: unit tests for the citation checks, the question filter, the secret scan, and the ledger chain.
- `make solo`: creates a second owner on the local server so one person can run the live two-owner flow.
- `make killtests`: reruns K1 and K2 against a local Switch server and writes `evidence/k1k2-*.log`.

## Limits

- "Verified" means the quote matches the session the author sealed at PR open.
  It does not prove the session file is genuine: an author who forges a session before opening the PR is out of scope.
  The tool targets authors who did not read what their agent did.
- Work done outside the agent has no session evidence, and the reviewer sees that.
- Authors without a coding agent get questions only.
- A verified chunk proves the quote is in the sealed session, not that it fully answers the question; the coverage line shows what was withheld.
- The seal proves consistency with the committed session, not that this session produced the code.
- Only the room owner can add an agent to a room (K1 found a 403 for the reviewer adding their own agent); the template run adds both agents.

## Status

| Item | State | Check |
|---|---|---|
| Two owners' agents in one room; reviewer refused by Switch; owner and scoped reviewer-agent admitted (K1, 6 checks) | pass | `killtests/k1_k2.py`, `evidence/k1k2-*.log` |
| Approval card answerable only by the author; agent receives the decision (K2, 3 checks) | pass | same |
| Approval card renders in Slack with buttons (K3) | not run | - |
| Seal, chunk proofs, structured questions, checks | not built | `make test` |
| Replay demo | not built | `make replay` |
| Fixture results (8 cases) | not run | `docs/results/fixtures.md` |

